Secure backend API request and response payloads using hybrid encryption. The Init API remains plain JSON and provides the encryption configuration required for subsequent API calls.

Encryption Trigger

Init API response contains networkSecurity.
If networkSecurity=true, all subsequent backend API calls must use encrypted request/response format.
If networkSecurity=false or missing, all subsequent API calls must use plain JSON.
If networkSecurity is missing in Init API response, default it to false.
If networkSecurity is present but not boolean, fail Init API response validation.
No local env flag should control encryption.
Init API Behavior

Init API request is always plain JSON.
Init API response must store/update:
accessToken
journeyId
publicKey
networkSecurity
Latest successful Init API response should replace the active values.
Init API should send plain JSON headers:
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
Encrypted Request Behavior

When networkSecurity=true:

Generate a fresh random AES key per request.
Encrypt request payload using AES-GCM-256.
Encrypt the AES key using backend-provided RSA public key.
Send request body as:
{
  "content": "base64(RSAEncryptedAESKey).base64(IV).base64(AESEncryptedPayload)"
}
Request headers must be:
Content-Type: application/v1.enc.hdfc+json
Accept: application/v1.enc.hdfc+json
Empty or no-body requests should be encrypted as {}.
Existing headers like Authorization, x-journey-id, and x-journey-name must continue to be sent.
Encrypted Response Behavior

When an encrypted request is sent:

Store the generated AES key in request metadata.
Use the same AES key to decrypt the matching response.
Expected encrypted response format:
{
  "content": "base64(IV).base64(AESEncryptedPayload)"
}
Decrypted response should be passed to the existing app/service processing flow.
Cipher and Key Details

Payload encryption: AES-GCM-256
AES key generation: random per request
AES IV: random 12-byte IV per encryption operation
AES-GCM authentication tag: 128-bit
AES key wrapping: RSA-OAEP with SHA-256
RSA public key source: Init API publicKey
RSA private key remains only with backend
Plain JSON Behavior

When networkSecurity=false or missing:

Requests should be sent without encryption.
Request body should remain normal JSON.
Headers must be:
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
Error Handling

If networkSecurity=true but publicKey is missing, stop the request and show common technical issue bottom sheet.
Bottom sheet copy:
Title: Opps! Something doesn't seem right.
Message: Looks like we're facing some technical issue at the moment.
If encryption fails, show technical issue bottom sheet.
For encrypted success responses, if decryption fails, show technical issue bottom sheet.
For encrypted non-2xx responses:
Try to decrypt { content } first.
If decryption fails, treat response as plaintext fallback because backend/proxy may accidentally return plaintext error.
Special Cases

Init API is always plain JSON.
Google reCAPTCHA token generation from Google is not encrypted.
Backend API call for reCAPTCHA verification follows the same networkSecurity rule.
IDCOM callback recovery should also store latest Init API networkSecurity and publicKey.
Acceptance Criteria

Init API stores networkSecurity with default false if missing.
Wrong networkSecurity type fails validation.
Settings API no longer contains or validates networkSecurity.
Encrypted requests use application/v1.enc.hdfc+json.
Plain requests use application/json.
AES key is generated per request and reused only for matching response decryption.
Non-2xx encrypted responses are decrypted before error handling, with plaintext fallback.
Existing app processing continues to receive decrypted/plain JSON transparently.
